doc_id: python-connection-pooling-patterns
title: Python Connection Pooling Patterns
project: ai-workflow
version_hint: "2024-2025"
kind: python_pooling_standards
description: Framework-agnostic Python patterns for connection pooling, resource lifecycle, and pool configuration applicable to any database or client library.
scope: Covers generic pooling fundamentals, pool size tuning, timeout handling, connection recycling, error handling patterns, testing strategies, anti-patterns, and monitoring. Technology-specific implementations are in surrealdb/, elasticsearch/, and fastapi/ modules.
out_of_scope[4]:
  - SurrealDB-specific pool implementation (see surrealdb/connection-pooling-patterns.toon).
  - Elasticsearch wrapper specifics (see elasticsearch/connection-pooling-patterns.toon).
  - FastAPI lifespan integration (see fastapi/connection-pooling-patterns.toon).
  - Repository APIs built on top of pools (see python/repository-patterns.toon).
primary_runtime_references[2]:
  - path: app/infrastructure/surrealdb/pool.py
    role: Example SurrealDB pool implementation demonstrating pooling patterns.
  - path: app/infrastructure/elasticsearch/client.py
    role: Example Elasticsearch wrapper demonstrating client lifecycle patterns.
related_pattern_docs[7]:
  - fastapi/connection-pooling-patterns.toon
  - surrealdb/connection-pooling-patterns.toon
  - elasticsearch/connection-pooling-patterns.toon
  - python/factory-patterns.toon
  - python/repository-patterns.toon
  - python/settings-patterns.toon
  - python/exception-patterns.toon
sections[8]:
  - id: connection-pooling-fundamentals
    index: 1
    title: Connection pooling fundamentals
    category: standard
    summary: Core principles for creating and managing pooled connections in Python applications.
    sample_code:
      description: Generic pool initialization pattern with singleton-like access.
      language: python
      code: |
        from typing import Protocol
        class ConnectionPool(Protocol):
            """Protocol defining the interface for connection pools."""
            async def init(self) -> None:
                """Initialize pool connections."""
                ...
            async def close(self) -> None:
                """Close all connections in the pool."""
                ...
            async def acquire(self):
                """Acquire a connection from the pool."""
                ...
        async def create_pool(settings) -> ConnectionPool:
            """Factory function that creates and initializes a pool."""
            pool = SomePool(dsn=settings.url, size=settings.pool_size)
            await pool.init()
            return pool
    items[5]:
      - id: fundamentals.startup-only
        type: rule
        text: Create connection pools and long-lived clients only once during application startup, before handling requests or processing tasks.
      - id: fundamentals.no-per-request
        type: rule
        text: Never create database pools, search clients, or HTTP clients inside per-request code paths; pool creation is expensive and leaks resources.
      - id: fundamentals.factory-pattern
        type: pattern
        text: Use factory functions such as create_pool(settings) to encapsulate pool construction, initialization, and configuration from a centralized Settings source.
      - id: fundamentals.singleton-management
        type: note
        text: Manage pool instances through composition roots or dependency injection rather than module-level globals; this enables testing and configuration flexibility.
      - id: fundamentals.access-via-injection
        type: note
        text: Access pools and clients through dependency injection so tests can provide mock implementations without modifying production code.
  - id: pool-size-tuning
    index: 2
    title: Pool size tuning
    category: standard
    summary: Guidelines for choosing and adjusting pool sizes.
    items[4]:
      - id: tuning.conservative-defaults
        type: rule
        text: Start with small pool sizes (for example 5-10 connections per pool or per node) instead of guessing large values; premature optimization wastes resources.
      - id: tuning.respect-backend-limits
        type: rule
        text: Ensure workers multiplied by pool size stays well below backend connection limits to avoid resource exhaustion on the database or search cluster.
      - id: tuning.observe-metrics
        type: note
        text: Monitor metrics such as acquire timeouts, queue wait times, request latencies, and error rates before changing pool sizes; data-driven tuning outperforms guesswork.
      - id: tuning.scale-incrementally
        type: pattern
        text: Increase pool sizes gradually and evaluate impact; prefer query optimization and caching where they provide better benefits than simply adding connections.
  - id: connection-timeout-handling
    index: 3
    title: Connection timeout handling
    category: standard
    summary: Patterns for using timeouts as signals of pool exhaustion or backend slowness.
    sample_code:
      description: Timeout handling with asyncio.wait_for.
      language: python
      code: |
        import asyncio
        import logging
        logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)
        async def acquire_with_timeout(pool, timeout: float):
            """Acquire a connection with explicit timeout handling."""
            try:
                conn = await asyncio.wait_for(pool.get(), timeout=timeout)
                return conn
            except TimeoutError:
                logger.warning("Timed out waiting for connection from pool")
                raise
    items[4]:
      - id: timeouts.acquire
        type: rule
        text: Use asyncio.wait_for or equivalent mechanisms to enforce acquire timeouts; log when callers cannot obtain connections within the configured window.
      - id: timeouts.request
        type: rule
        text: Bound operations with request-level timeouts to prevent hanging when backends are slow or unreachable.
      - id: timeouts.logging-alerts
        type: note
        text: Treat repeated timeouts as indicators of pool exhaustion or backend slowness; investigate query patterns, indexing, and infrastructure before simply raising pool sizes.
      - id: timeouts.fail-fast
        type: rule
        text: Translate timeout failures into well-typed exceptions so callers and exception handlers can convert them into appropriate error responses.
  - id: connection-recycling
    index: 4
    title: Connection recycling
    category: standard
    summary: Handling stale connections and long-lived pools.
    items[4]:
      - id: recycling.connection-lifetime
        type: note
        text: Allow pool connections to live for the process lifetime but be prepared to recreate them if servers restart or networks drop idle connections.
      - id: recycling.health-checks
        type: note
        text: Consider adding explicit health checks or recycling policies to pools to rebuild connections proactively when failures are detected.
      - id: recycling.client-features
        type: note
        text: Rely on client library features such as dead node detection and connection refreshing rather than manually closing and reopening connections.
      - id: recycling.http-backends
        type: pattern
        text: For HTTP-based backends, consider periodically recycling clients or clearing connection pools (for example every 30 minutes) to handle proxies and load balancers that silently drop idle connections.
  - id: pooling-error-handling
    index: 5
    title: Error handling
    category: standard
    summary: Handling startup, runtime, and shutdown errors for pooled resources.
    sample_code:
      description: Error handling pattern for pool initialization.
      language: python
      code: |
        import logging
        logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)
        async def create_pool_with_setup(settings):
            """Create pool with initialization and cleanup on failure."""
            pool = Pool(dsn=settings.url, size=settings.pool_size)
            await pool.init()
            try:
                await pool.initialize_schema()
            except Exception:
                logger.exception("Schema initialization failed")
                await pool.close()
                raise
            return pool
    items[5]:
      - id: errors.startup-fail-fast
        type: rule
        text: If pool or client creation fails during startup, log the error and re-raise it so the application does not start in a broken state.
      - id: errors.cleanup-on-failure
        type: pattern
        text: Ensure factories clean up partially initialized resources (for example closing opened connections) before propagating startup errors.
      - id: errors.runtime-metadata
        type: rule
        text: Log runtime pool errors and timeouts with enough metadata (operation type, pool name, key identifiers) to support troubleshooting.
      - id: errors.typed-exceptions
        type: pattern
        text: Have infrastructure layers raise well-typed domain exceptions so higher layers can translate them into appropriate error responses.
      - id: errors.shutdown-logging
        type: note
        text: During shutdown, log failures to close pools or clients but do not prevent process termination; best-effort cleanup is sufficient once traffic stops.
  - id: testing-connection-pools
    index: 6
    title: Testing with connection pools
    category: testing
    summary: Unit, integration, and end-to-end testing strategies for pooled resources.
    sample_code:
      description: Pytest fixtures for pool testing without real backends.
      language: python
      code: |
        import pytest
        from unittest.mock import AsyncMock, MagicMock
        @pytest.fixture
        def mock_pool():
            """Create a mock pool for unit tests."""
            pool = MagicMock()
            pool.acquire = AsyncMock(return_value=MagicMock())
            pool.close = AsyncMock()
            return pool
        @pytest.fixture
        def fake_connection():
            """Create a fake connection that captures queries."""
            conn = MagicMock()
            conn.query = AsyncMock(return_value=[])
            return conn
    items[5]:
      - id: test.unit-doubles
        type: rule
        text: Use fakes or test doubles for pools and clients in unit tests so they do not require real backends.
      - id: test.monkeypatch-factories
        type: pattern
        text: Monkeypatch pool factory functions to return dummy resources in tests that only care about validation or error behavior.
      - id: test.integration-fixtures
        type: pattern
        text: Use integration fixtures that create real pools against test databases or search clusters to exercise actual connection behavior.
      - id: test.e2e-live
        type: note
        text: Use end-to-end fixtures that hit a dockerized stack with real backends for full system validation.
      - id: test.avoid-real-in-unit
        type: rule
        text: Avoid contacting real databases or search clusters from unit tests; restrict such connectivity to integration and E2E suites.
  - id: pooling-anti-patterns
    index: 7
    title: Anti-patterns to avoid
    category: warning
    summary: Common mistakes when working with pools and long-lived clients.
    items[5]:
      - id: anti.per-request-pools
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Creating connection pools or HTTP clients inside route handlers or per-request code paths.
      - id: anti.bypassing-pool
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Opening new database or HTTP connections for every operation instead of reusing pool-managed connections.
      - id: anti.oversized-pools
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Configuring excessively large pool sizes without regard for backend limits, which can starve other services and hide performance problems.
      - id: anti.no-timeouts
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Failing to configure acquire or request timeouts, causing requests to hang when backends are slow or unreachable.
      - id: anti.no-cleanup
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Neglecting to close pools and clients during shutdown, leading to leaked sockets, file descriptors, or threads.
  - id: monitoring-and-security
    index: 8
    title: Monitoring and security considerations
    category: standard
    summary: Observability and security practices for pooled resources.
    items[4]:
      - id: monitoring.logs-metrics
        type: rule
        text: Emit structured logs for pool initialization, timeouts, acquire failures, and backend errors; record metrics for pool utilization, queue wait times, and backend latency where available.
      - id: monitoring.tuning-feed
        type: note
        text: Use monitoring signals such as timeouts, queue lengths, and error rates as primary input when tuning pool sizes, timeouts, and retry policies.
      - id: security.no-credential-logging
        type: rule
        text: Avoid logging raw connection strings, credentials, or hostnames; follow Settings conventions that mark sensitive fields as non-repr.
      - id: security.config-through-settings
        type: rule
        text: Store credentials and hosts exclusively in configuration surfaced via Settings and never hard-code them in code or tests.
